1 STARTUP

   What govax does each time it starts:

     1. Mounts the system volume on DUA0:, making it the first time (see
        SYSTEM_VOLUME).
     2. Mounts the containers the --mount and --mount-write options
        name, and the default volume (vax.default.volume.file), on DUA1:
        unless configured otherwise.
     3. Runs SYS$MANAGER:STARTUP.COM from the system volume, at command
        level 1, as a login command procedure.  It defines the system
        logical names, sizes the memory (INITIALIZE/VAX and
        INITIALIZE/MEMORY, from the vax.memory.* settings), loads
        SYS$SYSTEM:DEFAULT.ROM, assembles SYS$SOURCES:KERNEL.ASM and
        starts it, defines TTA0: and NLA0:, and last runs
        SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM.
     4. Installs the page file, with paging on.
     5. Runs vax.startup.procedure, if that names a procedure.  Like
        STARTUP.COM, it runs as SYSTEM, with every privilege.
     6. Logs process 1 in as its account: SYSTEM, or the account the
        --user option names, whose UIC, privileges, and quotas it takes
        from here on.  An account with a password must give it: govax
        asks at a terminal, and otherwise reads the GOVAX_PASSWORD
        environment variable.  The home directory becomes SYS$LOGIN and
        the default directory.
     7. In a session (not for a command on govax's command line), runs
        the login procedures: SYS$SYLOGIN (SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM),
        then the account's LGICMD, or SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM when it has
        none.  One that isn't there, or can't be read, is passed over.
     8. Runs the command on govax's command line and exits, or else
        shows the DCL $ prompt.

   Your own startup commands belong in SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:
   govax makes it once and never changes it.  Without a system volume
   (--system-file none, or one that can't be used), govax runs its
   built-in copy of STARTUP.COM instead.  See CONFIGURATION KEYS for the
   settings.

2 Time_zone

   govax's local time is the host's.  As it starts, it defines the
   logical name SYS$TIMEZONE_DIFFERENTIAL in the system table, in
   executive mode, as VMS V6 and later do: the local time's difference
   from UTC, as a signed count of seconds east of Greenwich, from the
   host's offset at that moment.  "-18000" is five hours west, US
   Eastern standard time; an offset east of Greenwich has no "+".

     $ SHOW LOGICAL SYS$TIMEZONE_DIFFERENTIAL
        "SYS$TIMEZONE_DIFFERENTIAL" = "-18000" (LNM$SYSTEM_TABLE)

   A change of the host's offset during a session (summer time) isn't
   followed; redefine the name, or start govax again.  FORTRAN's
   DATE_AND_TIME reads it through LNM$FILE_DEV for its ZONE and
   VALUES(4), so a process may define its own:

     $ DEFINE SYS$TIMEZONE_DIFFERENTIAL "3600"

   Without a number there, DATE_AND_TIME's ZONE is blanks.
